Ugly. Cleaner: don't track nextMode at all. Compute it only when transitioning:

```ts
handleReplayTimeout = async () => {
  const { replayTimeout } = this.props
  const { mode, replay, wellStateId } = this.state

  let nextReplayTimeoutId

  if (mode === 'REPLAYING') {
    await this.handleRedo()

    if (wellStateId + 1 in replay) {
      nextReplayTimeoutId = setTimeout(this.handleReplayTimeout, replayTimeout)
    } else if (this.state.mode === 'REPLAYING') {
      // The replay is over and the game isn't: hand control
      // back to the player
      this.setState({ mode: 'PLAYING' })
    }
  } else {
    console.warn('Ignoring input replay step because mode is', mode)
  }

  this.setState({
    replayTimeoutId: nextReplayTimeoutId
  })
}
```

Two setStates (batched by React 18+? Inside a timeout (not an event) — React 18's automatic batching batches all updates including timeouts. So they merge into one render.) This way I never touch mode except for the REPLAYING→PLAYING transition, and no overwrite risk.

Coverage: the else-if is the same as before.

