Cleanest fix: only set mode when the replay is exhausted, and never in the continuing branch. Two options:

Option A:
```ts
if (mode === 'REPLAYING') {
  await this.handleRedo()

  if (wellStateId + 1 in replay) {
    nextReplayTimeoutId = setTimeout(this.handleReplayTimeout, replayTimeout)
  } else if (this.state.mode === 'REPLAYING') {
    // The replay is over, but the game hasn't ended:
    // return control to the player
    this.setState({ mode: 'PLAYING' })
  }
}
...
this.setState({ replayTimeoutId: nextReplayTimeoutId })
```

Two setState calls, but — React 19 batches these (inside the async function, after await... does React 18+ auto-batch everywhere, including inside promises/timeouts? React 18's automatic batching covers setTimeout, promises, fetch — yes, all updates inside React event handlers, promises, and timeouts are batched). So the render only happens once. No problem.

Option B: single setState with mode only when exhausted:
```ts
const updates = { replayTimeoutId: nextReplayTimeoutId }
...
```
Conditional keys — less clean.

Option A is the minimal change from the original code. Original code:

